Found while measuring karr #122 / ADR 0040, which adds one
should_encrypt_path call per leaf to File::SOPS::_compute_macs sweep. It is
not that changes fault -- the call is the cheapest correct way to ask which
slot a leaf is going into -- but it doubles a cost that was already there.

MEASURED (perl 5.40.1, best of seven, this machine):

  File::SOPS::Metadata->should_encrypt_path([qw(database password)])
  with the default unencrypted_suffix          1.87 us per call

  File::SOPS->encrypt, 1000 string leaves      41.4 ms -> 43.0 ms  (+4%)
  File::SOPS->encrypt, 2000 leaves             98   ms -> 100  ms  (+2%)

_encrypt_tree already calls it once per leaf, and _mac_digest calls it once
per leaf again for a mac_only_encrypted document. So a 1000-leaf document now
pays 2-3 ms in this one predicate.

WHERE THE TIME GOES, measured separately: it is NOT mainly the regex
recompile. `grep { /\Q$self->{unencrypted_suffix}\E$/ } @$path` against a
precompiled qr// over the same two-element path is 0.84 us vs 0.72 us per
call, so ~0.12 us of the 1.87. The rest is the method call plus four
`defined ... && length ...` Moo accessor pairs, three of which are almost
always undef.

CANDIDATES, none measured yet: cache a per-metadata closure built once from
whichever of the four rules is actually configured (the common case is one
suffix and nothing else, i.e. one qr// and one grep); or have the walks share
one answer per leaf instead of asking twice.

NOT URGENT: a real secrets file has tens of leaves, where this is tens of
microseconds. It shows up on synthetic thousand-leaf documents.

API lane -- lib/File/SOPS/Metadata.pm.
